From 5767a920cc772a47b28acfc56895c9faeef5fd78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 23:14:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Material managers: padding of the arrays

The layouts of the material managers get a padding: a number of unused
values added to the leading dimension of the arrays of the gradients,
of the thermodynamic forces, of the internal state variables and of
the tangent operator blocks. The stride between two points becomes
nc + padding point by point, the stride between two components becomes
n + padding component by component. The energies, the speed of sound
and the values of the material properties and of the external state
variables are not padded.

This is a testing facility: the unused values of the arrays allocated
by the managers are quiet NaNs, so that a code computing the position
of a value by hand (i * nc + c, c * n + i) instead of using the views
reads NaNs and fails loudly. The arrays given by an initializer, or by
useExternalArrayOfTangentOperatorBlocks, must have the padded size: the
error message gives the expected size.

The internal code paths go through the views: allocation
(internals::allocateArray), views of the arrays and of the tangent
operator blocks, size checks, HDF5 save and restore (written and read
point by point through the views). The strided coalesced views of the
material function managers can't describe a padded array and are
refused with a clear message. The *_stride members and K_stride remain
the numbers of values of a point. The C function setting the layout of
all the arrays keeps the padding; the Python bindings expose it.
